# Daily check that what this repository says about segmentation is still true.
#
# WHY A TIMER AND NOT ONLY A DEPLOY-TIME CHECK. Every other check in this family
# watches something this repository changes: check_mounted_config.py catches a
# deploy that stranded a config, check_loki_coverage.py catches a rule this repo
# edited going blind. This one watches something this repository does NOT
# change. The ruleset lives in `Gerrrt/Lemmiwinks` and in the pfSense web UI, so
# the drift it hunts opens on a day when nothing here was deployed at all —
# which is exactly how it went unnoticed three times on 2026-09-06 (#363):
#
#   * ADR-0013's "default deny does not hold for the switch LAN" was four days
#     stale when #344 caught it by reading.
#   * Its correction said the switch LAN "still reaches every segment outbound",
#     false since 2026-09-02 (#229).
#   * ADR-0025 called `50 -> 30` and `50 -> 99` wholesale paths "that no rule
#     grants and no rule denies", four days after one was denied by name and the
#     other granted by name.
#
# All three were found by a person reading a file. Hanging this on `make up`
# alone would leave that as the only mechanism on any week without a deploy.
#
# WHAT IT ASKS. Per interface and address family: which other segments does the
# catch-all `pass ... to any` still reach, because no full-segment block sits
# above it. docs/firewall-claims.yaml declares the answer and this diffs it
# against `pfctl -a '*' -sr` read over SSH. It never writes a rule body anywhere
# — output names rules by descr only — which is what lets it run unattended
# without violating docs/security.md's "rule bodies are not published".
#
# WHY IT CANNOT BE CI, and this is the whole reason the check has this shape:
# the ruleset carries the WAN address and user password hashes, `backups/` is
# gitignored, and both `make validate` and CI assert nothing under it is tracked.
# Putting it in the repository so CI could read it would trade a documentation
# defect for a blueprint of the network. ADR-0026 records that; #363 built this.
#
# NEEDS SSH TO THE FIREWALL and nothing else — no age key, no docker, no lock.
# FW_HOST and FW_USER default to 10.0.99.1 and root, the same pair
# scripts/backup-firewall.sh uses, and override in /etc/default/homelab-timers.
#
# Failure here is a documentation finding, not an outage. Nothing is broken when
# it exits 1: the firewall is right and a document is stale. The fix is to
# re-derive the claims file and move the prose that cites it, never to edit an
# ADR in place — ADR-0001 makes those immutable.
#
# See homelab-backup-volumes.service for why each directive below is set.
[Unit]
Description=HomeLab: check firewall claims against the running ruleset
Documentation=file:///home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab/docs/runbooks/schedule-maintenance.md
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=robo
Group=robo
WorkingDirectory=/home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/default/homelab-timers
ExecStart=/home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab/scripts/run-scheduled.sh --job firewall-claims -- /usr/bin/make -C /home/robo/code/Gerrrt/HomeLab check-firewall
TimeoutStartSec=300
Restart=no
Nice=10
ProtectHome=false
ProtectSystem=false
PrivateTmp=true
NoNewPrivileges=true
RestrictSUIDSGID=true
LockPersonality=true
RestrictRealtime=true
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=homelab-firewall-claims
